1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,480
Welcome to another episode of Everyday Grammar on VOA Learning English.

2
00:00:08,320 --> 00:00:12,580
English learners have difficulty with gerunds and infinitives.

3
00:00:13,780 --> 00:00:20,180
A gerund is the ing form of a verb that functions the same as a noun.

4
00:00:21,460 --> 00:00:24,240
For example, running is fun.

5
00:00:25,340 --> 00:00:28,440
In this sentence, running is the gerund.

6
00:00:28,440 --> 00:00:31,660
It acts just like a noun.

7
00:00:33,120 --> 00:00:42,020
The infinitive form of a verb appears either as the basic form with no marking or with the word to.

8
00:00:42,980 --> 00:00:48,120
For example, you can say, I might run to the store.

9
00:00:49,100 --> 00:00:51,280
Or, I like to run.

10
00:00:52,700 --> 00:00:56,240
In this sentence, to run is the infinitive.

11
00:00:56,240 --> 00:01:05,220
It is difficult for English learners to know whether to use a gerund or an infinitive after a verb.

12
00:01:06,620 --> 00:01:07,620
Here's an example.

13
00:01:08,780 --> 00:01:10,420
Which sentence is correct?

14
00:01:12,100 --> 00:01:12,640
Sentence 1.

15
00:01:13,580 --> 00:01:15,920
I suggested going to dinner.

16
00:01:17,600 --> 00:01:18,220
Sentence 2.

17
00:01:19,560 --> 00:01:21,920
I suggested to go to dinner.

18
00:01:23,820 --> 00:01:27,460
Sentence 1 with the gerund is correct.

19
00:01:28,680 --> 00:01:30,580
I suggested going to dinner.

20
00:01:31,840 --> 00:01:32,100
Why?

21
00:01:33,220 --> 00:01:37,080
You can only use a gerund after the verb suggest.

22
00:01:38,480 --> 00:01:40,120
Let's take the word like.

23
00:01:41,120 --> 00:01:43,540
You can say, I like running.

24
00:01:44,400 --> 00:01:46,300
Or, I like to run.

25
00:01:47,660 --> 00:01:50,060
Both sentences have the same meaning.

26
00:01:50,060 --> 00:01:55,440
You can use either a gerund or an infinitive after like.

27
00:01:57,020 --> 00:01:58,600
Now let's try enjoy.

28
00:01:59,580 --> 00:02:02,420
We can say, I enjoy running.

29
00:02:03,420 --> 00:02:07,380
But we cannot say, I enjoy to run.

30
00:02:08,360 --> 00:02:08,680
Why?

31
00:02:09,660 --> 00:02:12,860
Only a gerund can follow the verb enjoy.

32
00:02:14,520 --> 00:02:16,040
Are you confused yet?

33
00:02:16,840 --> 00:02:17,840
You're not alone.

34
00:02:17,840 --> 00:02:23,880
Gerunds and infinitives confuse even very advanced English learners.

35
00:02:25,700 --> 00:02:31,540
Basically, some verbs are followed by gerunds, some verbs are followed by infinitives,

36
00:02:32,160 --> 00:02:36,740
and some verbs can be followed by gerunds or infinitives.

37
00:02:38,180 --> 00:02:41,100
Native speakers do not think about the difference.

38
00:02:41,840 --> 00:02:48,020
But English learners have to memorize the hundreds of different verb combinations.

39
00:02:49,480 --> 00:02:50,720
Here are a few tips.

40
00:02:52,000 --> 00:02:52,800
Tip number one.

41
00:02:53,460 --> 00:02:57,700
You almost always find a gerund after a preposition.

42
00:02:58,760 --> 00:03:02,500
For example, she is afraid of flying.

43
00:03:03,440 --> 00:03:09,040
In this sentence, of is the preposition, and flying is the gerund.

44
00:03:09,040 --> 00:03:14,120
You cannot say, she is afraid of to fly.

45
00:03:14,900 --> 00:03:19,320
An infinitive cannot be the object of a preposition.

46
00:03:19,940 --> 00:03:21,200
Only a gerund can.

47
00:03:22,240 --> 00:03:25,720
You could say, she is afraid to fly.

48
00:03:26,420 --> 00:03:30,620
But in this sentence, the preposition of is gone.

49
00:03:32,260 --> 00:03:33,120
Tip number two.

50
00:03:33,980 --> 00:03:38,800
When you are talking about an activity, you usually use a gerund.

51
00:03:38,800 --> 00:03:42,780
For example, I stopped smoking.

52
00:03:44,080 --> 00:03:48,640
You can describe many activities by using go before a gerund.

53
00:03:49,920 --> 00:03:53,400
Let's go shopping, or we went skiing.

54
00:03:54,800 --> 00:03:56,480
Let's see how much you know.

55
00:03:57,480 --> 00:04:01,620
Try to complete these sentences using the verb study.

56
00:04:02,580 --> 00:04:02,820
Ready?

57
00:04:03,660 --> 00:04:07,660
I'll read the first part of the sentence, and you finish it.

58
00:04:09,500 --> 00:04:13,020
I enjoy...

59
00:04:13,020 --> 00:04:13,200
studying.

60
00:04:14,840 --> 00:04:17,880
I considered...

61
00:04:17,880 --> 00:04:18,100
studying.

62
00:04:20,300 --> 00:04:23,100
I managed...

63
00:04:23,100 --> 00:04:23,600
to study.

64
00:04:24,660 --> 00:04:26,720
I hope...

65
00:04:26,720 --> 00:04:27,340
to study.

66
00:04:28,120 --> 00:04:30,860
I suggested...

67
00:04:30,860 --> 00:04:31,160
studying.

68
00:04:32,280 --> 00:04:34,800
I like...

69
00:04:34,800 --> 00:04:36,820
studying, or...

70
00:04:36,820 --> 00:04:37,220
to study.

71
00:04:39,440 --> 00:04:44,320
This is only a simple introduction to a complicated grammar topic.

72
00:04:46,100 --> 00:04:50,920
There is no quick and easy way to learn gerunds and infinitives.

73
00:04:51,760 --> 00:04:56,700
It takes years of practice and familiarity with the English language.

74
00:04:58,080 --> 00:05:02,920
Next time you read or listen to a VOA learning English story,

75
00:05:02,920 --> 00:05:07,280
pay attention to the use of gerunds and infinitives.

76
00:05:08,540 --> 00:05:12,660
Over time, you will begin to hear the right verb combination.

77
00:05:14,380 --> 00:05:18,900
Below is a helpful reference list for using gerunds and infinitives.

78
00:05:20,120 --> 00:05:20,960
I'm Jonathan Evans.

79
00:05:21,860 --> 00:05:23,080
And I'm Ashley Thompson.

80
00:05:25,400 --> 00:05:27,280
And now, words in this story.

81
00:05:28,200 --> 00:05:29,040
Gerund.

82
00:05:29,520 --> 00:05:29,780
Noun.

83
00:05:30,660 --> 00:05:34,380
An English noun formed from a verb by adding ing.

84
00:05:36,100 --> 00:05:36,940
Infinitive.

85
00:05:37,620 --> 00:05:37,760
Noun.

86
00:05:38,540 --> 00:05:40,220
The basic form of a verb.

87
00:05:41,040 --> 00:05:46,100
Usually used with to, except with modal verbs like should and could,

88
00:05:46,760 --> 00:05:50,040
and certain other verbs like see and hear.

89
00:05:51,780 --> 00:05:52,240
Preposition.

90
00:05:53,220 --> 00:05:53,340
Noun.

91
00:05:53,340 --> 00:05:59,580
A word or group of words that is used with a noun, pronoun, or noun phrase

92
00:05:59,580 --> 00:06:05,780
to show direction, location, or time, or to introduce an object.

93
00:06:07,020 --> 00:06:07,920
Now it's your turn.

94
00:06:08,640 --> 00:06:13,560
In the comments section, write one sentence that uses a verb followed by a gerund

95
00:06:13,560 --> 00:06:14,860
or an infinitive.

96
00:06:15,800 --> 00:06:18,660
We'll respond with feedback about your usage.

